droids, sea anemones, corals and some of the smallest marine parasites. Their distinguishing features are a decentralized nerve net distributed throughout a gelatinous body, and the presence of cnidocytes or cnidoblasts, specialized cells with ejectable organelles used mainly for envenomation and capturing prey.","revisionId":1375415561,"title":"Cnidaria","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cnidaria","wikidataId":"Q25441"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Ducks, Geese, And Swans","source":"Checklist of Vermont Species"},{"language":"en","name":"Ducks, Swans, and Geese","source":"Checklist of Vermont Species"},{"language":"en","name":"Ducks","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Geese","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Swans","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Dabbling Ducks","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Anatidae","confidence":94,"rank":"family","scientificName":"Anatidae","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2986},"ottId":765193,"rank":"family","scientificName":"Anatidae","synonyms":["Dendrocygnidae"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The Anatidae are the biological family of water birds that includes ducks, geese, and swans. The family has a cosmopolitan distribution, occurring on all the world's continents except Antarctica.","revisionId":1375849415,"title":"Anatidae","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anatidae","wikidataId":"Q7556"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Gymnogenes","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Harrier-Hawks","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Polyboroides","confidence":94,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Polyboroides A.Smith, 1829","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2480658},"ottId":837577,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Polyboroides","synonyms":["Poloboroides"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"Polyboroides is a genus of bird of prey in the family Accipitridae. This genus has two recognized species found in Sub-Saharan Africa an